  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nWidespread high cloud coverage can be expected across the area today \nas Tropical Storm Rachel quickly approaches Northern Baja. Radar\nis indicating areas of rain already with isolated thunderstorms\nover the coastal waters and San Diego mountains and deserts.  \nRainfall chances will increase from south to north tonight into \nSunday morning as Rachel makes landfall in northern Baja. Winds \nare also expected to begin to increase through the day continuing\ninto Sunday. Elevated winds over the mountains and into the \ndeserts are expected to continue into Monday morning. Most of the \nimpacts from Rachel are expected to occur between late tonight and\nearly Sunday afternoon, with conditions improving from south to \nnorth. Coastal flooding is expected to continue into early next \nweek due to continued elevated tides and lingering elevated surf. \nPlease see the Beaches discussion below for more information on \ncoastal flooding concerns. \n\nLight rain today will become moderate to heavy around midnight \nfor San Diego County. Based on the current forecast, most \nlocations will see their heaviest and most widespread rain before \nnoon, with some potential for moderate to locally heavy rain to \ncontinue into the early afternoon for portions of Riverside and \nSan Bernardino Counties. Rainfall totals of 0.40 to 1.25 inches \nare expected for the coast and valleys with highest totals for \nsoutheastern areas, 1.5 to 3.5 inches for the mountains, and 0.50 \nto 2 inches of rain for the deserts. Maximum rainfall rates of \n0.50-0.60 inches per hour for the coast and valleys, 0.50-0.70 \ninches per hour for the deserts, and 0.70-1 inch per hour in the \nmountains. Remain alert and heed advice of local officials as \nareas of flash flooding are possible tomorrow. The San Diego River\nat Fashion Valley is currently forecast to peak at 7.5 feet \n(action/monitor stage) around noon on Sunday. Those with interests\nnear the San Diego River should continue to monitor the forecast.\n\nWinds are also expected to increase tonight into Sunday. Tropical \nStorm force winds are still possible across San Diego county. \nOutside of tropical storm force wind potential the most likely wind \nforecast is  25-45 mph through the coastal basin and 50-70 mph near \nthe mountain ridges. Early Sunday, easterly winds expected for San \nDiego and Riverside county mountains, before turning \nsouthwest/westerly. Wind gusts for the coast and valleys are \nexpected to weaken to 20 to 25 mph and be less frequent for the \nafternoon, before weakening in the evening. Winds are expected to \nstay elevated from the west over all mountains and deserts into \nearly Monday morning.\n\nScattered rain showers will continue across the coastal basin into \nMonday. Drier and warmer conditions expected Tuesday through much of \nnext week. There is a potential for Santa Ana winds to develop for \nthe end of next week. Low confidence in details.",
